      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T16:42:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e This motivation is specifically about AI authorship, but we also include any other non-human contributor, such as bots and other automated tools.\n\u003e \n\u003e The exception for CI/CD automation bot is already excluded.\n\u003e Is there anything else that you believe we do today and should also be excluded from human authorship?\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e For example, an automated dependency update could reasonably use a bot as its technical Git author, with a human subsequently reviewing and approving the change.\n\u003e \u003e \n\u003e \u003e I don\u0027t think that should be prohibited, right?\n\u003e \n\u003e No, we don\u0027t do automated dependency updates because of Google\u0027s `Library-Compliance` requirement.\n\u003e \n\u003e Any other examples?\n\nI don\u0027t think we should forbid anything that does not already exist today, so I don\u0027t think it is relevant to just list _today\u0027s examples`.\n\nMy point was simply that, if this guide is specific to AI, referring to any non-human contributor in a generic way seems broader than what we intend to say.\n\nI don\u0027t feel strongly about this wording. Resolving.",
      "parentUuid": "204481ae_6ad16029",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 126,
