      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T18:00:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e If the generation means \"I don\u0027t have a clue because it was generated\" I fear that allowing us to simply tag the change as Assisted-By: AI could be used as a scapegoat for not taking responsibility.\n\u003e \n\u003e I didn\u0027t propose that the tag should lift responsibility.\n\n😎 therefore we are on the same page here: with or without the tag, the responsibility of the author should be exactly the same.\n\n\u003e `Assisted-by: AI` to me means: _AI assisted in preparing this change._\n\u003e It does not mean: _I am submitting this change and it is not my resposability_\n\nWell, who doesn\u0027t use AI in some form nowadays?\nEven just using IntelliJ is directly or indirectly using AI for suggesting what\u0027s the next part of the code to write.\n\n\u003e \u003e What do you mean by \"substantial\"? 10%? 20%? 50%?\n\u003e \n\u003e It has to be defined.\n\nWhy would it matter if it is 10% or 50%?\nAre you expecting the contributor to tag the source code parts that are developed with the assistance of AI?\n\nIf not, how can the reviewer know which part of AI-generated or human-generated?\n\n\u003e \u003e I have no objection to a generic Assisted-By: AI, but I do not understand its value.\n\u003e \n\u003e It can provide reviewers with common AI generated-code risks and transparency in understanding whether AI produced a significant portion of the codebase.\n\nThat\u0027s exactly my point: if the author foresee any risks in using AI generated-code, then he should not use it or bear those risks in his professional insurance.\n\nWith regard to the reviewer, it should not matter if the code has been initially generated by AI or not: all the code uploaded for review is expected to be of high quality and ready for review.\n\nI am very much against opening the door to AI-slops tagged with `Assisted-by: AI`. \n\n\u003e I agree it might be unnecessary overhead; however, I suggested it since I noticed it in other Google projects. Let\u0027s see if it is a Google requirement.\n\nSure, if Google mandates it, I do not see any benefits, but I do not have anything against it. I just do not find it useful.",
