      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e I think this section is a bit vague as it does not say what a contributor must actually do in this case.\n\u003e \n\u003e I find it very specific: it says that you can never assign authorship to the tool.\n\u003e _\"the tool cannot be credited as an author\"_ is an explicit statement. How would you phrase it for not being vague?\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e For example, suppose a developer uses an AI assistant to generate most of a new Java class, carefully reviews and modifies it, and submits the change under their own name.\n\u003e \u003e \n\u003e \u003e Is that compliant without explicitly disclosing the AI assistance? \n\u003e \n\u003e As long as the code is yours, you understand and agree, and you have verified that what was generated matches what you expected, then yes, I believe it is compliant.\n\u003e \n\u003e I believe everyone uses AI assistance in some shape or form: it is embedded in the IDE nowadays.\n\u003e The fact that your IDE or AI agent proposes some code doesn\u0027t mean that it is the author. You still have to read, understand, amend, and make the code yours in some shape or form.\n\u003e \n\u003e If you add the footer `Assisted-By: AI` in all your contributions, what is that adding to the reviewer?\n\u003e How should a reviewer react when looking at the footer?\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e I think we should explicitly state whether AI usage must be disclosed and, if so, where: commit message, change description, etc.\n\u003e \n\u003e I don\u0027t believe the point of these contribution guidelines is about adding an `Assisted-By: AI` footer as disclosure or using AI.\n\u003e I am using AI whilst writing this comment because it is integrated into my editing experience, and it constantly checks the sense of what I am writing and proposes fixes and more fluent ways of expressing myself.\n\u003e \n\u003e Should I also add `Assisted-By: AI` to every comment I write? I don\u0027t believe it would add anything to the communication.\n\u003e \n\u003e Whatever I am writing, as code or comments, remains mine, and I take full responsibility for it, whether it was assisted by AI or not.\n\n+1 agreed.\n\nHaving said that, I don\u0027t think _having_ the footer (whether it adds or not anything to the communication or not) shifts any responsibility, as it is already documented elsewhere, so I personally don\u0027t see it as harmful.\n\nI am curious to hear what the consensus on this is.",
