    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fbdef916_5b80d109",
        "filename": "Documentation/dev-contributing.txt",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 143,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T15:56:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Should we actually require every patch set to be manually submitted?\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, a developer could review and validate a generated change and then have a script push it to Gerrit. That doesn\u0027t make the contribution any less accountable.\n\nI believe the concept here is that we don\u0027t allow AI agents to automatically create and submit changes for review.\nThat would generate a huge workload of contributions of unclear provenance.\n\n\u003e I think the important distinction is whether the change has been reviewed and validated by a human, rather than whether the actual upload was manual.\n\nThe point here isn\u0027t about human review, but human authorship.\nThe code review *could actually be* the part fully automated by AI, and we are coding a lot of features in Gerrit to make it possible.\n\nThe whole point here is the authorship: humans are the authors, not AI.\nIf you are a human who writes a script to submit 10 patches that you have prepared locally, then it is a manual submission, even if the push has been done by a script.\n\nAt the end of the day, the human is the one who pushes the button, but it is always a program (or script) that runs the `git push` command, isn\u0027t it?\n\n\u003e Could we focus on prohibiting autonomous changes without human involvement, rather than prohibiting automated submissions in general? That would also avoid needing specific exceptions for CI/CD and release scripts, etc.\n\nThe CI/CD and release scripts are already excluded. Do you foresee other exclusions?",
      "parentUuid": "a91ac1dc_f1911c63",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 142,
        "startChar": 66,
        "endLine": 143,
        "endChar": 40
      },
